{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448135-publications-for-baroque-vtm","id":1448135,"title":"Publications for Baroque VtM","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-10T09:27:31-07:00","id":1448135,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Publications for Baroque VtM","content":"Greetings,<br /> <br /> are there any publications for playing VtM (regardless of edition) during the Early Modern period? I'm looking especially for the period that falls under the term Baroque/Rococo/Absolutism etc. (depending on your preferred terminological framework - I'm aware that they don't mean exactly the same, but I hope that my question is clear), so especially 17th century and early 18th century.<br /> <br /> I'd really like to play a V5 present-day campaign with older vampires, where I make excessive use of the Memoriam and where we play nearly 1/3 of the time during the Baroque time period with a plot revolving around J.S. Bach.<br /> <br /> I will probably make up most of the stuff but I'd really like to know, which hitherto publications touch the time period. It seemed like there was some big gap between V: DA and the Victorian Era.<br /> <br /> Thank you all in advance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:georgia\">&quot;Life's barely long enough to get good at one thing. So be careful what you get good at.&quot;</span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26196-saturno","name":"Saturno","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26196_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"48"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-10T12:13:52-07:00","id":1448159,"title":"","content":"I think there aren't vtm books for this time period. The modern sects will have largely supplanted, but not entirely replaced the old ways of doing things, so use your modern corebook but maybe sprinkle in some dark-age bubbles. The best thing to be looking at is RL history, at least what clothes people wore and how they lived and maybe check your city of choice isnt going through anything tumultuous that you don't wanna miss out on. Vampires don't really change much, but their backdrop does.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-10T16:26:43-07:00","id":1448174,"title":"","content":"I think there may be some parts of the Transylvania Chronicles or the Giovanni Chronicles which take place during the period, but probably only tangentially to where you're planning to focus on. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-10T23:27:36-07:00","id":1448203,"title":"","content":"I want to say the part of Giovanni Chronicles II set in London occurs in the mid 1600s.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T08:47:01-07:00","id":1448221,"title":"","content":"The Giovanni Chronicles took place during the great fire of London (1666), but probably, you will find even more interesting reading the lore of some characters: Madame de Guill, the Baron Volgirre, Fran\u00e7ois Villon and the Count Germaine have and intertwined plot during the French Revolution. In the end, the place is as many important as the era, because it would be not the same the Siglo de oro of Spain than the colonization of Canada.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T09:20:00-07:00","id":1448231,"title":"","content":"Thank you all for your input. It feels like ages past when I held the Giovanni Chronicles in my hand. But yes, I will probably look those NPCs up. Apart from that I will probably plan the chronicle pretty close to human history and politics.<br /> <br /> The place will probably be the Holy Roman Empire (maybe Prussia and Saxe-Eisenach) but shorter sessions in Austria or France might also be nice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:georgia\">&quot;Life's barely long enough to get good at one thing. So be careful what you get good at.&quot;</span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26196-saturno","name":"Saturno","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26196_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"48"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T12:46:14-07:00","id":1448254,"title":"","content":"Back between the World Wars, one of the more popular film genres was what became known as Prussian Films. They were usually set in (and lionized) the court of Frederick the Great. Unfortunately they are almost impossible to find these days, outside of clips and stills that crop up online. Which is a shame because the costumes and set design were often very impressive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T13:04:59-07:00","id":1448257,"title":"","content":"You might get some additional data by reading the history of individual characters found in various books. They often refer to historical events even though that time period is not really emphasized. Chicago By Night has several characters who lived during the time period you wish. Annabelle Treabelle was born in 1698 and embraced in 1722, and was born to a musician and Parisian prostitute. The Sabbat Phillipe Rigaud was born 1650 and embraced in 1673 was a French soldier w ho participated in Louis XIV's invasion of Holland in the Franco-Dutch War. We know the Brujah elder Critias was active in Europe doing this time, but probably spent most of his time then at a Swiss monastery.<br /> <br /> The Nosferatu Alexander Danov was embraced possibly around the time of the Battle of Tannenberg in 1410, though he did not pick up his current name until the mid to late 19th century in Russia far after your time period. We know the ancient Gangrel Lucian must have existed during that time, but his history is an entirely blank slate during the period.<br /> <br /> Whether any of these characters would play a role in your chronicle, is complete personal choice. But they could definitely be used or name dropped during your chronicle. Is this kind of thing helpful to you? I have no idea.<br /> <br /> But the details are there provided you want to read up on a lot of old By Night books, or any of the other supplements that had lots of NPCs in them. There's a significant number of elders who were either embraced during that time period, or were already elders by that era. Berlin By Night, while a rather terrible sourcebook over all, does have some native German vampires who would have existed during that time period (and lots with no association with Germany or way after that time period). After all, J.S. Bach visited the court of Frederick the Great.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T14:40:23-07:00","id":1448261,"title":"","content":"For Deutschland and Osterreich during the ancient regime and the Holy Roman Empire, I would recommend reading the books of Feder &amp; Schwert. They are not entirely canon, but some of their characters are, as the Vienna governors of the Clan Tremere and the Justicar Ulugh Begh. I would also recommend you Berlin by night (although many consider it the worst of the 15 by nights). Gustav Breidenstein was a powerful Prince and an important political player. Finally, I could imagine Hardestadt reverenced in the Holy Roman Empire as the ruler he was during the Black Cross Fiefdoms era.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Wiener_Blut:_Wien_bei_Nacht\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Wi...Wien_bei_Nacht</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Gustav_Breidenstein\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Gustav_Breidenstein</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Ulugh_Begh\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Ulugh_Begh</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Karl_Schrekt\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Karl_Schrekt</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T17:43:37-07:00","id":1448271,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448135-publications-for-baroque-vtm?p=1448254#post1448254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Back between the World Wars, one of the more popular film genres was what became known as Prussian Films. They were usually set in (and lionized) the court of Frederick the Great. Unfortunately they are almost impossible to find these days, outside of clips and stills that crop up online. Which is a shame because the costumes and set design were often very impressive.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you mean the Fridericus Rex movies? I've seen some of them. They're quite compelling visually and maybe nice for the players to set the tone of the chronicle although some of the movies themselves are a bit creepy politically.<br /> <br /> But I guess, I could sample some scenes from the earlier Fridericus Rex movies with some background music and a bit of story exposition into a trailer for the chronicle. Nice one. Good suggestion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448135-publications-for-baroque-vtm?p=1448257#post1448257\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You might get some additional data by reading the history of individual characters found in various books. They often refer to historical events even though that time period is not really emphasized. Chicago By Night has several characters who lived during the time period you wish. Annabelle Treabelle was born in 1698 and embraced in 1722, and was born to a musician and Parisian prostitute. The Sabbat Phillipe Rigaud was born 1650 and embraced in 1673 was a French soldier w ho participated in Louis XIV's invasion of Holland in the Franco-Dutch War. We know the Brujah elder Critias was active in Europe doing this time, but probably spent most of his time then at a Swiss monastery.<br /> <br /> The Nosferatu Alexander Danov was embraced possibly around the time of the Battle of Tannenberg in 1410, though he did not pick up his current name until the mid to late 19th century in Russia far after your time period. We know the ancient Gangrel Lucian must have existed during that time, but his history is an entirely blank slate during the period.<br /> <br /> Whether any of these characters would play a role in your chronicle, is complete personal choice. But they could definitely be used or name dropped during your chronicle. Is this kind of thing helpful to you? I have no idea.<br /> <br /> But the details are there provided you want to read up on a lot of old By Night books, or any of the other supplements that had lots of NPCs in them. There's a significant number of elders who were either embraced during that time period, or were already elders by that era. Berlin By Night, while a rather terrible sourcebook over all, does have some native German vampires who would have existed during that time period (and lots with no association with Germany or way after that time period). After all, J.S. Bach visited the court of Frederick the Great.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, you've been helpful thank you. It's a bit tiresome to read every NPC just because there <i>might</i> be some information. So thank you for dropping some names. I'm mostly not heavy on using established lore characters (which is mostly because VtM tends to be pretty focused on the US and most of our chronicles play in Europe) and I very much enjoy establishing my own NPCs (I also make playing cards with portraits of them (that are supposed to fit the VtM style) and with 2-3 important character traits. (I could upload them, if someone's interested.)<br /> But in this case I might use a small number of established NPCs aditionally.<br /> So, again, thanks. I will look them up.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justycar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448135-publications-for-baroque-vtm?p=1448261#post1448261\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For Deutschland and Osterreich during the ancient regime and the Holy Roman Empire, I would recommend reading the books of Feder &amp; Schwert. They are not entirely canon, but some of their characters are, as the Vienna governors of the Clan Tremere and the Justicar Ulugh Begh. I would also recommend you Berlin by night (although many consider it the worst of the 15 by nights). Gustav Breidenstein was a powerful Prince and an important political player. Finally, I could imagine Hardestadt reverenced in the Holy Roman Empire as the ruler he was during the Black Cross Fiefdoms era.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Wiener_Blut:_Wien_bei_Nacht\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Wi...Wien_bei_Nacht</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Gustav_Breidenstein\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Gustav_Breidenstein</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Ulugh_Begh\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Ulugh_Begh</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Karl_Schrekt\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Karl_Schrekt</a></div> </div> </div> </div>Tbh, I found Wiener Blut to be okay. But I passionately hate Berlin bei Nacht. It's one of the most awkward and uninformed takes on german history I've ever seen in an RPG. In our chronicle the whole book is non-canonical, although Breidenstein was prince of Berlin and stayed prince until the Anarchs took Berlin recently and most of the Camarilla moved to Potsdam and holds court in Sanssoucci. (I especially didn't like the take on Nazi history, which was pretty uninformed and also not very usable from a narrative standpoint in my opinion.)<br /> But yes, Breidenstein and Schrekt are canon in our group (and Schrekt was one of the main antagonists in our last chronicle).<br /> <br /> I'm really thankful for the big amount of feedback. Thank you all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:georgia\">&quot;Life's barely long enough to get good at one thing. So be careful what you get good at.&quot;</span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26196-saturno","name":"Saturno","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26196_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"48"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T18:15:56-07:00","id":1448277,"title":"","content":"Yeah, Berlin by Night was ... &quot;a terrible waste of potential&quot; probably sums it up best. Berlin in the early 90s was a fantastic place to set a Vampire game, with neonates being influenced by the various music scenes in both parts of the city, and memories of life under the Stasi, and ancilla being from the Weimar and Imperial eras (and even a few who remember the last days of the Holy Roman Empire), and elders who remember the Teutonic Knights or even Charlemagne or Rome. Instead it was this crazy alternate dimension where WW2 was the only thing of any historical importance which occurred in the city. And vampire snakes. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Speaking of the Weimar period, I will again express my love for Chaosium's Berlin: The Wicked City sourcebook for Call of Cthulhu.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-12T02:46:23-07:00","id":1448305,"title":"","content":"I agree with you about Berlin bei Nacht. There was also another source of inspiration, the Germany chapter of Dark Ages Europe. Julia Antasia and Hardestadt probably were still plotting against each other. Supossedly Julia was the sire of Barbarossa.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Die_Stadt,_Das_Blut,_Der_Tod:_Frankfurt_bei_Nacht\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Di...furt_bei_Nacht</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Julia_Antasia\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Julia_Antasia</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24202-justycar","name":"Justycar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24202_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"320"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-12T05:24:39-07:00","id":1448314,"title":"","content":"Aetas Baroca. Vampire around 1666 (in spanish).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://mega.nz/#!gZx3gL5Q!tzedmdd6cDYw159J1SowUP3e-LVM5vXOqWw0fe94IQs\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://mega.nz/#!gZx3gL5Q!tzedmdd6cDYw159J1SowUP3e-LVM5vXOqWw0fe94IQs\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://mega.nz/#!gZx3gL5Q!tzedmdd6cDYw159J1SowUP3e-LVM5vXOqWw0fe94IQs\" target=\"_blank\"> https://mega.nz/#!gZx3gL5Q!tzedmdd6cDYw159J1SowUP3e-LVM5vXOqWw0fe94IQs </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2250-magus","name":"Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T09:16:25-07:00","id":1448696,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1448135-publications-for-baroque-vtm?p=1448314#post1448314\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Aetas Baroca. Vampire around 1666 (in spanish).<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://mega.nz/#!gZx3gL5Q!tzedmdd6cDYw159J1SowUP3e-LVM5vXOqWw0fe94IQs\" target=\"_blank\">https://mega.nz/#!gZx3gL5Q!tzedmdd6c...vXOqWw0fe94IQs</a></div> </div> </div> </div>Awesome, thank you. I never learned Spanish, but I can (very slowly) read it and understand the general gist. I might use some of that. Many, many thanks.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:georgia\">&quot;Life's barely long enough to get good at one thing. So be careful what you get good at.&quot;</span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26196-saturno","name":"Saturno","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26196_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"48"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}